```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  weights |>
    filter(cell %in% c("p1", "p2", "p3", "p4")) |>
    mutate(arm = factor(arm, levels = arm_levels)) |>
    select(cell, arm, w_nec4param) |>
    pivot_wider(names_from = cell, values_from = w_nec4param) |>
    arrange(arm),
  col.names = c("Convention", "p1 (CV 9.6%)", "p2 (CV 6.7%)",
                "p3 (CV 2.8%)", "p4 (CV 1.9%)"),
  digits = 3,
  caption = "**Table 2.** Mean stacking weight on the generating equation `nec4param` across the precision sweep, averaged over 100 realisations per cell. The remaining weight is spread over the other eleven to thirteen equations in the candidate set."
)
```
Given the measurements as recorded, model averaging identifies the generating
equation, and identifies it more sharply as the experiment improves:
`nec4param` takes `r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p1" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
of the weight at a 9.6% control coefficient of variation and
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
at 1.9%. Under `censored` and `deleted` it still takes
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "censored"])`
and `r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "deleted"])`
of the weight at the highest precision, with most of the remainder on `neclin`,
which shares the same breakpoint and differs only in the shape below it.

Under the conventions that replace the measurement the generating equation
receives essentially none of the weight, and what replaces it is a
zero-asymptote shape. At the highest precision `floored` gives `nec4param`
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`
and gives `ecxsigm`
`r f2(weights$top_weight[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`;
`gamma` gives `ecxwb1`
`r f2(weights$top_weight[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "gamma"])`.
Both of those equations decay smoothly to zero, which is what floored data look
like, and the more precise the experiment the more confidently the weights say
so. A model weight is evidence about which shape fits the data supplied, not
about which process produced the measurements, and on floored data those are
different questions.

The six equations whose lower asymptote is fixed at zero are the clearest
signature of the substitution, and they identify it only where the substitution
is the thing imposing the boundary. At the highest precision they take
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`
of the weight under `floored`, against
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
under `measured` and
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "censored"])`
under `censored`. Under `beta` and `gamma` they take only
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "beta"])`
and
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "gamma"])`,
because there the family already forbids a negative mean and an equation with a
free lower asymptote is bounded below in any case. The weight on these
equations is therefore a diagnostic for flooring under an unbounded family, not
for the zero boundary in general.

# 6 Worked case studies

The simulation establishes which conventions recover the truth. It cannot show
how large the difference is on a real dataset, where the truth is unknown and
the design, the noise and the shape of the response are not under the analyst's
control. We therefore analysed four marine microalgal growth tests by all six
conventions, and compared each estimate with the `measured` estimate on the
same dataset.

The four tests ship with `bayesnec` as `alga`, so everything in this section is
computed when the vignette is built rather than transcribed: two tests on
*Cladocopium proliferum*, a coral endosymbiont, over seven days, and two on
*Rhodomonas salina* over three days, each species exposed to two contaminants
on undisclosed and mutually incomparable concentration scales.

```{r set-backend}
# cmdstan compiles each Stan program once and caches the executable, which
# matters here because the six conventions are averaged over twelve to fourteen
# equations each on four datasets. See the Installation section of the Hurdle
# and zero-inflated models vignette for setting it up.
options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")
# Four chains on four cores. Every fit in this section is independent of every
# other, so the only parallelism worth having is within a fit.
options(mc.cores = 4)
```

```{r}
data(alga)

# The four case studies are the four species-by-contaminant cells of `alga`.
# The names used throughout this section are the vignette's; the mapping is by
# cell rather than by row order, so it survives any reordering of the data.
alga$dataset <- factor(
  paste0(as.character(alga$species), ifelse(alga$contaminant == "B", "2", "")),
  levels = c("c_proliferum", "c_proliferum2", "r_salina", "r_salina2"))

# The growth rate implied by the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, against the
# starting density recorded for the test. Where the source substituted a zero
# for a below-limit count -- flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` -- this is the
# rate the substitution implies, and it is what the retaining conventions use in
# place of the substituted zero.
alga$bound <- (log(10) - log(alga$density_initial)) / alga$days
alga$below_limit <- alga$sgr_source == "substituted"

datasets <- alga |>
  group_by(dataset, species, duration_d = days) |>
  summarise(n = n(), n_conc = n_distinct(dose),
            control_rate = mean(sgr[dose == 0]),
            below_limit = sum(below_limit),
            prop_at_or_below_zero = mean(sgr <= 0), .groups = "drop") |>
  mutate(fold_change_R = exp(control_rate * duration_d), .before = below_limit)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Transposed: nine columns against four rows reads badly, and the datasets are
# the natural columns because every later figure compares across them. Each
# value is formatted before transposing -- t() coerces to character, so kable's
# `digits` no longer applies once the table is on its side.
datasets_t <- with(datasets, data.frame(
  Species = as.character(species),
  Days = as.character(duration_d),
  n = as.character(n),
  Concentrations = as.character(n_conc),
  `Control rate (per d)` = sprintf("%.3f", control_rate),
  `Fold change R` = sprintf("%.1f", fold_change_R),
  `Below limit` = as.character(below_limit),
  `Prop. at or below zero` = sprintf("%.2f", prop_at_or_below_zero),
  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE))
datasets_t <- as.data.frame(t(datasets_t), stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
names(datasets_t) <- datasets$dataset
knitr::kable(datasets_t,
  caption = "**Table 3.** The four case-study datasets, computed from `alga`. `Below limit` counts the rows whose population fell below the counting limit, for which the source substituted a zero. All four have a substantial proportion of observations at or below zero growth, so all four are in the regime where Section 5 applies.")
```

## 6.1 Undefined growth rates in the *Rhodomonas* tests

At the highest concentrations in both *Rhodomonas* tests the population fell
below the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, so the density is recorded as zero
and the specific growth rate is *undefined* rather than negative. Those rows
are flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` in `alga`, 16 on `r_salina` and 4 on
`r_salina2`. The two *Cladocopium* tests have none, and their negative values
are measurements.

There is no measured value to retain in those rows, so every convention must
substitute something. The `measured` convention uses the growth rate implied by the
counting limit, which is itself a substitution, and on those two datasets it is
therefore not a true analysis of the measurements. The comparison is
correspondingly weaker there. This is a common situation with real algal data
and one of the reasons left-censoring is useful: it can express "at or below
the limit" without inventing a value.

It also determines what `censored` means on those two datasets. Left-censoring
declares that the truth lies at or below a bound, so the bound should be the
tightest one the measurement supports: zero for a growth rate that was measured
and came out negative, but the counting limit itself for a row that fell below
it, which is a stronger statement than "below zero" and is the one thing the
test does establish about that row.

## 6.2 Estimates from each convention

Each dataset is prepared by each convention exactly as in Section 5.1 and then
passed to `bnec()` with package defaults, so the case studies and the
simulation run the same six conventions.

```{r, results = "hide"}
fit_arms <- function(d) {
  # The retaining conventions restore the counting-limit rate where the source
  # substituted a zero. On the two Cladocopium tests nothing is substituted and
  # this is the measured series unchanged.
  d$y <- ifelse(d$below_limit, d$bound, d$sgr)

  d_floored <- transform(d, y = pmax(y, 0))
  d_deleted <- d[d$y >= 0, , drop = FALSE]
  # For a measured negative the tightest defensible bound is zero -- withdrawing
  # the magnitude is the convention under test. For a row whose population fell
  # below the counting limit it is the limit itself; censoring such a row at
  # zero would discard the one thing the test does tell you about it. See the
  # Limits of the censored likelihood section of the Single model usage
  # vignette.
  d_cens <- transform(d,
    cens = ifelse(below_limit | y < 0, "left", "none"),
    y = ifelse(below_limit, bound, pmax(y, 0)))
  d_beta <- transform(d_floored, y = y / max(y))

  gauss <- function(dd, form = y ~ crf(dose, "decline")) {
    bnec(form, data = dd, family = gaussian(link = "identity"), seed = 333)
  }
  list(
    measured = gauss(d),
    censored = gauss(d_cens, y | cens(cens) ~ crf(dose, "decline")),
    deleted  = gauss(d_deleted),
    floored  = gauss(d_floored),
    beta     = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = d_beta,
                    family = Beta(link = "identity"), seed = 333),
    gamma    = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = d_floored,
                    family = Gamma(link = "identity"), seed = 333))
}

real_fits <- lapply(split(alga, alga$dataset), fit_arms)
```

```{r}
# ErC50 and NSEC from every fit, with the tested concentration range recorded
# alongside so that an interval running to the edge of the design can be
# flagged rather than read as an estimate.
real_estimates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits), function(ds) {
  rng <- range(alga$dose[alga$dataset == ds & alga$dose > 0])
  do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits[[ds]]), function(a) {
    f <- real_fits[[ds]][[a]]
    e <- ecx(f, ecx_val = 50, type = "absolute")
    n <- nsec(f)
    rbind(data.frame(dataset = ds, arm = a, estimate = "ErC50",
                     val = e[1], lo = e[2], hi = e[3]),
          data.frame(dataset = ds, arm = a, estimate = "NSEC",
                     val = n[1], lo = n[2], hi = n[3])) |>
      transform(x_min = rng[1], x_max = rng[2])
  }))
}))

# Expressed as a ratio to the `measured` analysis on the same dataset, because
# the four tests are on undisclosed and mutually incomparable concentration
# scales. An estimate is flagged unusable where its interval has collapsed to a
# point or has run to an edge of the tested range, where the bound is the design
# rather than the data.
real_ratios <- real_estimates |>
  group_by(dataset, estimate) |>
  mutate(ref = val[arm == "measured"],
         r = val / ref, rlo = lo / ref, rhi = hi / ref,
         # An estimate that could not be identified comes back NA rather than
         # erroring, and counts as unusable here: a convention that fails to
         # return a value on a real dataset is a result, not a gap in the table.
         usable = !is.na(r) & !is.na(rlo) & !is.na(rhi) &
                  !(lo <= x_min | hi >= x_max |
                      hi - lo < .Machine$double.eps^0.5)) |>
  ungroup() |>
  select(dataset, arm, estimate, r, rlo, rhi, usable)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# The weight each fit gives the equations that hold the lower asymptote at zero,
# which is the quantity Section 5.6 reports on the simulated data. Computed here
# so the case studies can be checked against it rather than assumed to agree.
zero_asymptote <- c("nec3param", "ecxexp", "ecxsigm", "ecxwb1p3", "ecxwb2p3",
                    "ecxll3")
real_weights <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits), function(ds) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(names(real_fits[[ds]]), function(a) {
    f <- real_fits[[ds]][[a]]
    # A model-averaged fit reports one weight per surviving equation. Where only
    # one equation survives, `bnec()` returns a single fit with no `mod_stats`,
    # and that equation holds all of the weight.
    w <- if (inherits(f, "bayesmanecfit")) {
      setNames(f$mod_stats$wi, rownames(f$mod_stats))
    } else {
      setNames(1, f$model)
    }
    data.frame(dataset = ds, arm = a,
               w_zero_asymptote = sum(w[names(w) %in% zero_asymptote]),
               top_model = names(w)[which.max(w)],
               top_weight = max(w))
  }))
}))
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Every figure the prose below quotes is computed here rather than typed, so a
# refit cannot leave the text describing an earlier one.
rr <- function(ds, a, est = "ErC50", what = "r") {
  real_ratios[[what]][real_ratios$dataset == ds & real_ratios$arm == a &
                        real_ratios$estimate == est]
}
zw <- function(ds, a) real_weights$w_zero_asymptote[
  real_weights$dataset == ds & real_weights$arm == a]
# The range across every convention other than the reference, and the single
# convention lying furthest from it on a log scale. Computed over all five
# rather than over the three that replace the measurement: on these datasets the
# retaining conventions do not always sit with the reference, and quoting only
# the replacing ones would imply a separation the data do not show.
others <- setdiff(arm_levels, "measured")
# Both drop an estimate that could not be identified rather than propagating it:
# a missing value is reported by `unusable_note()` above, and letting it through
# here would turn every quoted range into NA.
rng_ratio <- function(ds, est = "ErC50") {
  range(sapply(others, rr, ds, est), na.rm = TRUE)
}
furthest <- function(ds, est = "ErC50") {
  v <- sapply(others, rr, ds, est)
  v <- v[is.finite(v) & v > 0]
  if (!length(v)) return(NA_real_)
  unname(v[which.max(abs(log(v)))])
}
# Written as a computed phrase rather than a bare count: which estimates are
# flagged depends on the fits, "0 of 48" reads badly, and a number typed into
# the prose goes stale silently the next time the vignette is precompiled.
unusable_note <- function() {
  n <- sum(!real_ratios$usable)
  m <- sum(is.na(real_ratios$r))
  if (n == 0) {
    return(paste("No estimate in Figure 6 is flagged unusable: every interval",
                 "lies inside the tested concentration range."))
  }
  s <- sprintf(paste("%d of the %d estimates in Figure 6 are flagged unusable,",
                     "their intervals having reached an edge of the tested",
                     "concentration range or collapsed to a point"),
               n, nrow(real_ratios))
  if (m > 0) {
    s <- paste0(s, sprintf(", and %d could not be identified at all", m))
  }
  paste0(s, ".")
}
pc <- function(x) sprintf("%.0f%%", 100 * x)
```

Because the four datasets have toxicity values on completely different
concentration scales, each estimate is shown as a ratio: the convention's
estimate divided by the `measured` estimate on the same dataset. A ratio of 1
is exact agreement.

```{r real-ratios, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5.5, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 6.** Estimated ErC50 and NSEC from each convention on the four real datasets, expressed as a ratio to the `measured` analysis of the same dataset, with 95% credible intervals. The vertical line at 1 is exact agreement. Open symbols mark estimates flagged as unusable, meaning the interval reached an edge of the tested concentration range, where the bound is the design rather than the data, or collapsed to a point. Intervals extending past the plotted range are clipped at the panel edge."}
real_ratios$arm <- factor(real_ratios$arm, levels = rev(arm_levels))
# Clipping by pmax/pmin on the data rather than a scale limit, to avoid
# depending on `scales` for an out-of-bounds handler. An interval that runs past
# the panel is already flagged unusable and is drawn with an open symbol.
ratio_lim <- c(0.1, 4)
real_ratios$rlo_p <- pmax(real_ratios$rlo, ratio_lim[1])
real_ratios$rhi_p <- pmin(real_ratios$rhi, ratio_lim[2])

ggplot(real_ratios, aes(x = r, y = arm, colour = arm, shape = usable)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_errorbar(aes(xmin = rlo_p, xmax = rhi_p), orientation = "y",
                width = 0, linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_point(size = 2.2, fill = "white") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c("TRUE" = 16, "FALSE" = 21), guide = "none") +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4), limits = ratio_lim) +
  facet_grid(estimate ~ dataset) +
  labs(x = "Estimate as a ratio to the `measured` analysis (log scale)",
       y = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "none")
```

`r unusable_note()`

Three features of Figure 6 require care in what they do and do not establish.

The choice of convention changes the reported value substantially, and on one
of the four datasets it changes it in the same direction for every convention
at once. On `c_proliferum` all five conventions other than `measured` return an
ErC50 between `r f2(rng_ratio("c_proliferum")[1])` and
`r f2(rng_ratio("c_proliferum")[2])` times the `measured` estimate. These are
not rounding differences; they are the difference between one regulatory value
and another.

The size of that difference varies between datasets and cannot be predicted
from the data alone. On the other three datasets the convention lying furthest
from the `measured` estimate on ErC50 is at
`r f2(furthest("c_proliferum2"))` times it on `c_proliferum2`,
`r f2(furthest("r_salina"))` on `r_salina` and
`r f2(furthest("r_salina2"))` on `r_salina2`. Nor does the departure fall only
on the conventions the simulation identifies: on `c_proliferum` the two
retaining conventions are displaced with the rest. A single real dataset cannot
separate the effect of a convention from the error of the analysis it is being
compared against, because the `measured` analysis of that dataset has its own
error and there is no truth to score either of them by.

The weight on the zero-asymptote equations is the diagnostic Section 5.6
identifies for flooring under an unbounded family, and it is computable here
because it needs no truth.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  real_weights |>
    mutate(arm = factor(arm, levels = arm_levels)) |>
    select(dataset, arm, w_zero_asymptote) |>
    pivot_wider(names_from = dataset, values_from = w_zero_asymptote) |>
    arrange(arm) |>
    select(arm, all_of(levels(alga$dataset))),
  digits = 2,
  col.names = c("Convention", levels(alga$dataset)),
  caption = "**Table 4.** Total model weight on the six equations whose lower asymptote is fixed at zero, for each convention on each dataset. In the simulation at the highest precision tested this weight is 0.98 under `floored` and 0.00 under `measured` and `censored`."
)
```

Averaged over the four datasets `floored` puts
`r pc(mean(sapply(names(real_fits), zw, "floored")))`
of its weight on those equations, against
`r pc(mean(sapply(names(real_fits), zw, "measured")))`
under `measured` and
`r pc(mean(sapply(names(real_fits), zw, "censored")))`
under `censored`. The separation is in the direction the simulation gives and
is much smaller. Two things differ. In the simulation the generating equation
is in the candidate set and is known to be correct, so a retaining convention
recovers it and a floored one visibly cannot; on real data no candidate is
known to be correct. And these growth-rate curves decline toward zero whatever
is done to the data, so a zero-asymptote shape is a defensible description of
all four datasets rather than an artefact of one convention. The case studies
establish that the choice of convention changes the reported value. They do not
confirm the mechanism, and without a known truth they cannot.

# 7 Recommendations

Analyse the growth rates as measured and let the model set decide the shape.
Nothing in a Gaussian concentration-response fit requires the response to be
positive. In the simulation this recovers the true ErC50 to within half a per
cent at every level of measurement precision, holds nominal interval coverage,
and gives the generating equation
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
of the model weight at the highest precision tested.

If the negative values were never recorded, declare them censored rather than
substituting a value. At the highest precision tested it matches the
measurements themselves on ErC50, `r f1(val("censored", "ErC50", "p4"))`%
against `r f1(val("measured", "ErC50", "p4"))`%, and it is the only option when
the underlying values are gone. It is also the one
convention that holds nominal coverage for all three toxicity estimates across
the whole precision sweep.

Deleting the negative rows is better than replacing them and worse than keeping
them. Its ErC50 bias converges on zero as the experiment improves and its
coverage stays nominal, but it changes the replication per concentration and at
the top of a series it can remove a treatment group altogether, which is a
change to the design rather than to the data.

Do not replace negative values with zero. Flooring leaves an ErC50 bias of
`r f1(val("floored", "ErC50", "p4"))`% that does not shrink as the experiment
improves, so its interval coverage falls to
`r f2(val("floored", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))` at the highest precision
tested, and it displaces the NSEC by
`r f1(val("floored", "NSEC", "p4"))`%. Averaging over the enlarged candidate
set absorbs part of the distortion — the ErC10 result is close to unbiased —
but it does not remove it, and it cannot, because the floored data are no
longer draws from the process that produced the measurements.

Do not choose a zero-bounded response distribution to solve the problem. This
is the most common response to negative growth rates and it does not work. The
Beta holds an ErC50 bias near
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC50", "p4"))`% at every precision and an ErC10 bias that
grows to `r f1(val("beta", "ErC10", "p4"))`% as the experiment improves. The
Gamma is unbiased on ErC50 and displaces ErC10 by
`r f1(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p4"))`%, with coverage of
`r f2(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p4", "coverage"))`. Neither gives any outward sign
of trouble: every one of the 1,400 fits in those two conventions converged and
returned all three toxicity estimates.

Do not convert to percent inhibition before fitting. Expressing the response as
a percentage of the control caps it at 100%, which makes negative growth look
like an error and pushes the analyst toward a bounded family.
`ecx(type = "absolute")` already expresses the effect relative to the fitted
control, so the transformation is unnecessary as well as harmful; @Ritz2026
reach the same conclusion by a different route. `bayesnec` will warn if it
detects data that appear to have been normalised.

Report the proportion of observations at or below zero. This is the single
number that tells a reader whether the question arises for the dataset at all.
In the `ctl` cell, where about two observations in seventy are negative, the
four Gaussian conventions span `r f1(spread("ErC50", "ctl"))` percentage points
of ErC50 bias between them; at one in seven and the same control variability
they span `r f1(spread("ErC50", "p1"))`.

Do not report interval coverage, or interval width, alone. At the highest
precision tested `gamma` reaches nominal coverage on ErC50 with intervals
`r f2(val("gamma", "ErC50", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "ErC50", "p4", "width"))`
times as wide as those of `measured`, while `floored` reports narrower
intervals than `gamma` and covers the truth a quarter of the time. Either
figure on its own reverses the ranking.

Read the model weights as a description of the data supplied. On floored data
the generating equation receives essentially none of the weight and a
zero-asymptote shape receives almost all of it, with a confidence that
increases as the experiment improves. A weight is evidence about which shape
fits the data in front of the model, not about which process produced the
measurements.

# 8 Limitations

The data are generated from one equation. `nec4param` is in every Gaussian
convention's candidate set, so the study measures how well each convention lets a
correctly specified candidate be recovered. It does not establish what happens
when no candidate is correct, which is the ordinary situation with real data.

The residual scale is constant along the curve and every fit is homoscedastic
to match. That is a deliberate simplification, and it removes a confound that
was present in the earlier version of this study, but it also means the
dispersion sub-model is never exercised. One of the four `alga` datasets does
show a residual gradient along the curve, so the homoscedastic case is not the
only one that arises. Re-running the sweep under `disp("loglinear")` is tracked
in issue #283.

The priors are `bnec()`'s own defaults, derived once per cell and convention
from a reference realisation and then held fixed across the 100 realisations of
that cell, so that the Stan programs are identical between realisations and
compile once. Holding them fixed removes realisation-to-realisation prior
jitter, which is nuisance variation; each convention still keeps its own prior
derived from its own prepared data, so the contrast between conventions is
unaffected. Two properties of those defaults constrain what the study can
claim. The default `nec` prior places the true `nec` of 1.3 between the 6th and
the 82nd percentile depending on the cell, which is unremarkable. The same
family of prior applied to the `ec50` parameter places the true ErC50 above the
99th percentile in every cell, which is tight. Equations parameterised by
`ec50` take about a fifth of the weight under the conventions that retain the
measurement but
86% to 87% of it under `beta` and `gamma`, so some part of
the displacement reported for those two could be prior-driven rather than
convention-driven. The reported ErC50 is read off the fitted curve by
`ecx(type = "absolute")` rather than from the `ec50` parameter, which weakens
but does not remove that route. This is the first thing to check if these
results change under a revised default prior.

Each cell was simulated 100 times. The Monte Carlo standard error of the
reported bias is a median of 0.4% of the true value for ErC50, 1.2% for ErC10
and 2.1% for the NSEC, reaching 11.6% for the `gamma` NSEC in the `d2` cell.
Differences smaller than those are not resolved by this study. The Monte Carlo
standard error on a coverage of 0.95 is 0.02.

The design is fixed at seventy observations across twelve concentrations
spanning a factor of one hundred, with ten controls. Nothing here establishes
how the conventions behave on a smaller design, an arithmetic series, or one
that does not reach the zero crossing at all beyond the single `ctl` cell.

The case studies compare conventions on four datasets whose truth is unknown.
They show that the choice changes the reported value, and Section 6.2 records
where they do not reproduce what the simulation shows. They are not
recommendations for how those
particular tests should be analysed, and the *Rhodomonas* comparison is weaker
than the *Cladocopium* one because no convention there is a true analysis of
the measurements, as Section 6.1 records.

# 9 Provenance of the reported results

The simulation in Section 5 is not run when this vignette is built. It is 4,200
model-averaged fits over 52,533 individual equation fits, run as a job array on
a cluster, which is far more machine time than a package build can use. It is a separate research compendium,
[open-AIMS/negative-response-conventions](https://github.com/open-AIMS/negative-response-conventions)
[@negresponse2026], which contains the generating code, the runner, the
container definition pinning every package version, the fixed priors as
tracked files, and the result tables.

Every figure quoted from the simulation is transcribed from that compendium's
output at the commit named in the bibliography entry. The metrics table is
`results/metrics_100iter.csv` and the model weights of Table 2 are derived from
the per-unit records under `results/<cell>/`. The fits were run against
`bayesnec` at commit `0976caca`, version 2.1.3.33, built into the container
defined by `hpc/negative-response-conventions.def`. That is a development
commit rather than a CRAN release, so the default priors used are those of the
2.2.0 development line rather than of 2.1.3.1.

The case studies in Section 6 are computed when the vignette is built, from the
`alga` data that ship with `bayesnec`, so they track the current version of the
package while the simulation figures describe it as it behaved at that commit.

